  "theory": {
    "intro": "An exponential equation has its unknown in the index, like $2^{x}=32$. There is no operation that peels a variable out of an index directly, but there is a way in: if both sides can be written as powers of the same base, then their indices must be equal. That turns the exponential equation into an ordinary equation you can already solve.",
    "facts": [
      "If $a^{m}=a^{n}$ (with $a>0$ and $a\\ne1$) then $m=n$ — equal powers of the same base have equal indices.",
      "Rewrite both sides using the **smallest possible base**: $25^{x}=125$ becomes $5^{2x}=5^{3}$, not $25^{x}=25^{1.5}$.",
      "$\\left(a^{m}\\right)^{n}=a^{mn}$, so a bracketed index multiplies through every term: $9^{2x-1}=\\left(3^{2}\\right)^{2x-1}=3^{4x-2}$.",
      "A reciprocal on one side is a negative index: $3^{x}=\\frac{1}{9}=3^{-2}$, and $\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^{x}=3^{-x}$.",
      "A root is a fractional index: $\\sqrt{81}=3^{2}$ and $\\sqrt[5]{125}=5^{\\frac{3}{5}}$.",
      "Rewrite a decimal as a fraction first: $0.125=\\frac{1}{8}=2^{-3}$.",
      "$a^{0}=1$, so an equation whose right-hand side is $1$ gives an index of zero.",
      "The solution does not have to be a whole number — $9^{x}=27$ gives $x=\\frac{3}{2}$."
    ],
    "steps": [
      "Rewrite both sides as powers of the smallest possible base.",
      "Equate the indices and solve the resulting equation."
    ]
  },
